## 3. AI and Scraping Restrictions

### No AI Training

You may not use the Software, its source code, or its documentation to train,
test, evaluate, validate, or fine-tune artificial intelligence models, machine
learning algorithms, neural networks, or large language models.

### No AI-Related Scraping

You may not use automated bots, scrapers, spiders, or data mining tools to
extract, ingest, or index the Software into datasets, databases, vector stores,
or retrieval systems used for AI generation, model training, cloud-based code
completion, or similar technologies. Standard, purely local static analysis and
syntax completion performed within a code editor solely for a human developer's
reading or compilation of the Software are permitted, provided no code or data
is transmitted to external AI services or used to train or update any model.

### Restrictions on AI-Assisted Derivatives

You may not submit the original Software, its source code, or its documentation
to AI prompts, code generators, or AI services to create modified versions,
ports, translations, or other derivative works. Any permitted modified version
remains subject to the source-sharing and redistribution requirements below.

### Explicit Authorization by the Copyright Holder

The Copyright Holder may explicitly authorize a person, tool, or service to
perform otherwise restricted activities, including AI-assisted development,
review, maintenance, and documentation work on the Copyright Holder's behalf.
Authorization may be given in writing, including a direct project instruction
or conversation message, and applies only to the activities and scope expressly
authorized. Authorization to assist with the project does not by itself permit
model training, fine-tuning, unrelated scraping, or use for other projects.
It does not grant the same exception to anyone else.

### Machine-Readable Opt-Out Notice (TDM Reservation)

The restrictions in this Section 3 constitute an express reservation of rights
against text and data mining (TDM), web harvesting, and AI training under
Article 4 of the European Union Directive 2019/790 on Copyright in the Digital
Single Market (DSM Directive) and any equivalent national or international
copyright laws.

